---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import Plate from '../components/Plate.astro'

import hours from '../assets/plates/detail-hours.png'
import quitting from '../assets/plates/detail-quitting.png'
import plan from '../assets/plates/plan-top.png'
import heatmap from '../assets/plates/detail-heatmap.png'
import weekdays from '../assets/plates/detail-weekdays.png'
import challenge from '../assets/plates/detail-challenge.png'
import today from '../assets/plates/today.png'
---

<Layout
  title="Some Kaizen — habits with a place in the day"
  description="A habit tracker that gives every habit an hour on a real day, beside the sleep and work already on it. Offline, on your phone, with no account and no server."
>
  <section class="sheet opening">
    <div class="opening__say">
      <h1>Give a habit an hour, not a checkbox.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Some Kaizen puts the hours already spoken for on the day first — sleep, work, the
        commute — and then lets you drag a habit onto one that is actually free.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="act" href="https://github.com/yebt/some-kaizen" rel="noopener">
          Read the source
        </a>
        <a class="act act--quiet" href="#what">See what it does</a>
      </div>
      <p class="opening__status">
        Android · free · open source · no account · nothing leaves the device
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="opening__plate">
      <Plate
        src={hours}
        alt="The day timeline at seven in the morning: a shaded band of sleep ending, a card reading Meditate standing on the seven o'clock line, and a shaded band of work beginning at nine."
        loading="eager"
        callouts={[
          {
            x: 52,
            y: 12,
            toX: 104,
            toY: 6,
            what: 'Sleep is on the day, not around it.',
            why: 'Blocks of time are things you already do. They cannot overlap each other, and a plan is checked against them rather than against an empty grid.',
          },
          {
            x: 74,
            y: 47,
            toX: 104,
            toY: 44,
            what: 'The habit stands on an hour.',
            why: 'Dragged there, resized by its lower edge. An occurrence that never gets an hour is still valid — it simply happens sometime that day.',
          },
          {
            x: 26,
            y: 87,
            toX: 104,
            toY: 92,
            what: 'Work begins underneath it.',
            why: 'Habits may overlap a block freely, because reading on a commute is a real plan and an app that refused it would be wrong about your life.',
          },
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="sheet band" id="what">
    <div class="spread">
      <div>
        <h2>Three shapes, not one.</h2>
        <p class="margin-note">
          Most trackers have a single shape — done or not — and bend everything into it. Water
          becomes a tick. Quitting becomes a tick you press on a day you did nothing. Three
          separate shapes mean an impossible habit cannot be written down in the first place.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cols">
        <article>
          <h3>Did it</h3>
          <p class="quiet">
            Binary, and honest about it. You meditated or you did not, and the day says which.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Measured</h3>
          <p class="quiet">
            Carries a unit, a minimum and a goal. Reaching the minimum grades as a partial day,
            because most of your water is not the same as none of it.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Quitting</h3>
          <p class="quiet">
            Never planned and never performed. Each finished day is judged the following
            morning, which is the only moment the answer is actually known.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pair">
      <Plate
        src={quitting}
        alt="A row reading Smoking, dated yesterday, asking did you avoid it, with Yes and No buttons."
      />
      <p class="quiet pair__note">
        A quitting habit asks about <em>yesterday</em>. Asking at four in the afternoon whether
        you will smoke tonight is asking somebody to predict themselves, and the answer costs
        nothing to give and means nothing once given.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="sheet band">
    <div class="spread">
      <div>
        <h2>The week settles itself.</h2>
        <p class="margin-note">
          Three runs a week is a real intention, and Monday, Wednesday, Friday is a different
          one. Both are describable here — and only one of them leaves you anything to decide.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="plate-row">
        <Plate
          src={plan}
          alt="The weekly board: a chip reading Run waiting for a day, and Tuesday holding Meditate at seven, Stretch at half past seven, Run at half past six and Read at half past nine."
          callouts={[
            {
              x: 22,
              y: 22,
              toX: 108,
              toY: 14,
              what: 'Waiting for a day.',
              why: 'A habit that asks for three times a week sits here until you say which days. Drag it onto one and it is settled.',
            },
            {
              x: 62,
              y: 66,
              toX: 108,
              toY: 74,
              what: 'A day that already owes something.',
              why: 'Named days settle themselves. Nobody has to drag Monday, Wednesday and Friday onto Monday, Wednesday and Friday.',
            },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ground ground--sunken">
    <div class="sheet band">
    <div class="spread">
      <div>
        <h2>Statistics that will not flatter you.</h2>
        <p class="margin-note">
          A number that only ever goes up is decoration. These are built to be able to say
          something you would rather not read — and to say nothing at all when they do not yet
          know.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="stats">
        <div class="stats__heat">
          <Plate
            src={heatmap}
            alt="Six months of days as small squares: green where the habit was done, amber where it was partly done, pale where nothing was answered."
            callouts={[
              {
                x: 64.8,
                y: 7,
                toX: 105,
                toY: 12,
                what: 'A day never answered stays pale.',
                why: 'Not red, and not counted as zero — silence. A week you did not open the app has to read as a week of silence, or the calendar turns into a wall of defeat you stop opening.',
              },
              {
                x: 57.4,
                y: 35.5,
                toX: 105,
                toY: 62,
                what: 'Amber is a partial day.',
                why: 'The minimum reached, the goal missed. Most of your water is not the same as none of it, and the record keeps the difference instead of rounding it away.',
              },
            ]}
          />
        </div>

        <div class="plate-row plate-row--flat">
          <Plate
            src={weekdays}
            alt="Seven bars, one per weekday, from Thursday at 100 percent down to Saturday at 50, and the sentence beneath them naming the best day and the worst."
            callouts={[
              {
                x: 79,
                y: 58,
                toX: 106,
                toY: 32,
                what: 'Saturday is half of Thursday.',
                why: 'Fifty points apart, and that is the most actionable number a tracker can produce: a streak says how long you kept something up, this says what to change.',
              },
              {
                x: 57,
                y: 88,
                toX: 106,
                toY: 74,
                what: 'It only says it once it knows.',
                why: 'Named over the days that carry an answer, never over every day in the window — and withheld entirely until enough of them do, because one good Tuesday is not a pattern.',
              },
            ]}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sheet band">
    <div class="spread">
      <div>
        <h2>The rule, before you agree to it.</h2>
        <p class="margin-note">
          75 Hard says miss one day and you go back to day one. That contradicts everything
          else here, so it lives in its own container with its own rules and leaves the habits
          underneath alone.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="plate-row plate-row--narrow">
        <Plate
          src={challenge}
          alt="A card reading 75 Hard, day 3 of 75, above five unticked tasks."
          callouts={[
            {
              x: 60,
              y: 18,
              toX: 110,
              toY: 6,
              what: 'Every one today, or back to day one.',
              why: 'Said while it is still true, and it stops being said once the day is safe. A programme that explained its rule only after applying it would be a trap.',
            },
            {
              x: 20,
              y: 62,
              toX: 110,
              toY: 78,
              what: 'All of them, or none.',
              why: 'Three of five at six in the evening is a real state and the screen shows it, but the day counts only when the list is finished.',
            },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!--
    The page's second peak, and the only inverted band on it.

    This is the most opinionated content the site has — four things the product refuses and
    the reason for each — and it was set in exactly the same grey as everything around it.
    Inverted, it reads the way it means: a page you turn to and know you have arrived at
    something. The tokens are the app's own, swapped, the way its primary button has always
    used them.
  -->
  <section class="ground ground--ink">
    <div class="sheet band">
      <div class="headline headline--ruled">
        <h2>Four things it refuses to do.</h2>
        <p>
          Not an oversight list. Each of these was considered and turned down, and the reason
          matters more than the absence.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="ledger">
        <li>
          <span class="ledger__what">
            No points, levels or badges
            <span class="ledger__why quiet">
              A reward attached to something you already wanted reduces how much you want it
              once the reward stops — and points lost for a relapse make lying the rational move.
            </span>
          </span>
          <span class="ledger__value">refused</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="ledger__what">
            No account, no sign-up
            <span class="ledger__why quiet">
              There is nothing to sign in to. The data is in your phone's own storage and the
              app has no server to send it to.
            </span>
          </span>
          <span class="ledger__value">structural</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="ledger__what">
            No streak you can break
            <span class="ledger__why quiet">
              Days accumulate. A missed day is information, not an alarm, and it does not delete
              the ones behind it.
            </span>
          </span>
          <span class="ledger__value">by design</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="ledger__what">
            No adverts, no tracking, no analytics
            <span class="ledger__why quiet">
              The app makes no network requests of any kind. Neither does this page.
            </span>
          </span>
          <span class="ledger__value">none</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sheet band">
    <div class="spread">
      <div>
        <h2>Tools that need no install.</h2>
        <p class="margin-note">
          Built on the same code as the app, so they cannot quietly disagree with it. They hold
          their state in your browser and send nothing anywhere.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="ledger tools">
        <li>
          <span class="ledger__what">
            <a href="/tools/ideas">Habit ideas</a>
            <span class="ledger__why quiet">
              Small, ordinary, finishable habits, and the reason each one is worth doing. The
              same list the app offers, read out of its own source.
            </span>
          </span>
          <span class="ledger__value">open →</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="ledger__what">
            Heatmap generator
            <span class="ledger__why quiet">
              Tick days, export a picture of the year to keep or to post.
            </span>
          </span>
          <span class="ledger__value">planned</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="ledger__what">
            Routine planner
            <span class="ledger__why quiet">
              A wake-up time and a list of steps, cascaded into real hours.
            </span>
          </span>
          <span class="ledger__value">planned</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="sheet band closing">
    <div class="headline">
      <h2>Where it is, honestly.</h2>
      <p>
        Written down rather than implied, because a page that leaves this out is hoping you will
        assume something better.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="closing__grid">
        <div class="closing__body">
          <ul class="ledger">
          <li>
            <span class="ledger__what">Android, wrapped with Capacitor</span>
            <span class="ledger__value">working</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="ledger__what">iOS</span>
            <span class="ledger__value">no build exists</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="ledger__what">
              A downloadable build
              <span class="ledger__why quiet">
                Not published yet. The APK goes to the repository's releases first, and a store
                listing after that.
              </span>
            </span>
            <span class="ledger__value">not yet</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="ledger__what">
              Sync between devices, and hosted backup
              <span class="ledger__why quiet">
                Planned, and it would be paid — running a server costs money and pretending
                otherwise is how free things start reading your data instead. It will be
                something you opt into. Nothing about the offline app depends on it. No price
                and no date exist yet.
              </span>
            </span>
            <span class="ledger__value">planned, paid</span>
          </li>
          </ul>

          <div class="closing__act">
            <p>
              Until there is something to install, the honest thing to offer is the code. All
              of it, including the parts that explain why each decision went the way it did.
            </p>
            <div class="actions">
              <a class="act" href="https://github.com/yebt/some-kaizen" rel="noopener">
                Read the source
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <figure class="closing__plate">
          <Plate
            src={today}
            alt="The Today screen: a week strip, a 75 Hard programme part way through its tasks, and a row still to answer."
            device
          />
          <figcaption class="footnote">
            Today, in the app. Every picture on this page is a photograph of it running — on a
            worked example built for the purpose, never on anybody's own record.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .opening {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    padding-block: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 6rem) clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5.5rem);
    align-items: center;
  }

  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .opening {
      /* The thesis plate takes the larger half. The whole argument of a field guide is its
         photographs, and this one had never once been allowed to be the big thing. */
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.04fr);
      gap: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem);
      align-items: stretch;
      min-height: min(80vh, 44rem);
    }
  }

  .opening__say {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.75rem;
    justify-items: start;
    align-content: center;
  }

  /*
   * Capped a little below the page's own maximum, now that the plate takes the larger half.
   * At full size the line breaks four ways and leaves "habit an" hanging, which is a phrase
   * nobody says; three lines break where the sentence does.
   */
  .opening h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.75rem, 6.4vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .opening__status {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  /*
   * On the wide spread the left column runs the full height of the plate and its notes, so
   * the quiet facts line anchors the lower-left corner instead of leaving it empty — the two
   * columns finish on the same baseline.
   */
  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .opening__say {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
      padding-top: clamp(1rem, 4vh, 3.5rem);
    }

    .opening__status {
      margin-top: auto;
      padding-top: 1.75rem;
    }
  }

  /* The first plate is a crop, so it wants a frame rather than a device edge. */
  .opening__plate {
    /* Room in the right margin for the pins to stand outside the shot. */
    padding-right: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
  }

  .band {
    padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 5.5rem);
  }

  .cols {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 44rem) {
    .cols {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 2.5rem;
    }
  }

  .cols h3 {
    padding-bottom: 0.625rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--gold);
  }

  .cols p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.75rem;
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    align-items: center;
  }

  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .pair {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 22rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    }
  }

  .pair__note {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    max-width: 46ch;
  }

  /*
   * The pins stand outside the photograph rather than on top of it. A number sitting on the
   * interface it is pointing at hides the pixel the sentence is about, which is the one thing
   * this whole arrangement exists to avoid.
   *
   * On the wide spread the notes stand beside the shot rather than under it, so the leader
   * lines run out of the photograph, through the gutter where the pins wait, toward the notes
   * they belong to — and the field has no empty right half.
   */
  .plate-row {
    --plate-w: 24rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.75rem;
  }

  .plate-row > :global(.plate) {
    max-width: var(--plate-w);
    margin-right: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 5rem);
  }

  .plate-row--narrow {
    --plate-w: 22rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .plate-row {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, var(--plate-w)) minmax(0, 1fr);
      column-gap: clamp(4rem, 6vw, 5.5rem);
      align-items: start;
    }

    .plate-row > :global(.plate) {
      margin-right: 0;
    }

    .plate-row > :global(.notes) {
      margin-top: 0.25rem;
    }
  }

  .stats {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  /* The heatmap is wide and shallow, so it takes the whole field and its notes run under it. */
  .stats__heat > :global(.plate) {
    margin-right: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 5rem);
  }

  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .stats__heat > :global(.notes) {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      column-gap: 2.5rem;
    }
  }

  /* The weekday bars keep the row form: shot on the left, its note across the gutter. */
  .plate-row--flat {
    --plate-w: 26rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .plate-row--flat {
      align-items: center;
    }
  }

  .ledger__what {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.375rem;
  }

  .ledger__why {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    max-width: 58ch;
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .closing__body {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2.5rem;
  }

  .closing__act p {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .closing__grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  @media (min-width: 62rem) {
    .closing__grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 16rem;
    }
  }

  .closing__plate {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.875rem;
    max-width: 16rem;
  }

  .closing__plate figcaption {
    max-width: 30ch;
  }

  .tools :global(a) {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration-color: var(--gold);
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .tools :global(a:hover) {
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }
</style>
